{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"main-text","type":"text","text":"**Original Text Segment**\n\nDeploying artificial intelligence algorithms using non-volatile memory devices at the edge still faces numerous challenges at both the software and hardware levels to meet the complex and decentralized requirements of edge device environments.","x":0,"y":0,"width":600,"height":400,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"scale-challenge","type":"text","text":"**Challenge: Crossbar Array Scale**\n\nThe computational power of non-volatile memory devices for in-memory computing is directly related to the scale of their vector-matrix multiplication, i.e., the size of the crossbar array. Unfortunately, due to factors such as integration reliability and the complexity of read/write operations, the scale of ferroelectric transistor arrays remains constrained.","x":700,"y":0,"width":600,"height":300,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"write-crosstalk","type":"text","text":"**Challenge: Write Crosstalk**\n\nWrite crosstalk is a particularly prominent issue. Although ferroelectric transistors, as three-terminal devices, can separate read and write operations, they are still affected by the same wordline during the writing process. Existing methods (Vw/2, Vw/3, target programming) cannot fundamentally address write errors due to partial polarization characteristics.","x":1400,"y":0,"width":600,"height":350,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"hardware-implementation","type":"text","text":"**Hardware Implementation Challenges**\n\nExisting research has proposed many optimization methods for continual learning paradigms. However, when implemented in hardware, these approaches often require additional hardware overhead or complex operational strategies.","x":1400,"y":400,"width":600,"height":300,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"paper1","type":"text","text":"[1] [[Benchmarking Test-Time DNN Adaptation at Edge with Compute-In-Memory]]\n\nFan Z et al., ACM JATS 2024\n\n**Supports**: Edge deployment challenges, on-device adaptation necessity, CIM benchmarking","x":0,"y":800,"width":550,"height":200,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"paper2","type":"text","text":"[2] [[CMOS-compatible compute-in-memory accelerators based on integrated ferroelectric synaptic arrays for convolution neural networks]]\n\nKim MK et al., Science Advances 2022\n\n**Supports**: Scale-performance relationship, 4×4 array VMM implementation, 90.3% accuracy","x":600,"y":800,"width":550,"height":200,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"paper3","type":"text","text":"[3] [[Write Disturb in Ferroelectric FETs and Its Implication for 1T-FeFET AND Memory Arrays]]\n\nNi K et al., IEEE EDL 2018\n\n**Supports**: Write crosstalk analysis, Vw/2 and Vw/3 evaluation, array size limitations","x":1200,"y":800,"width":550,"height":200,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"paper4","type":"text","text":"[4] [[A Compact Writing Scheme for the Reliability Challenges in 1T Multi-Level FeFET Array: Variation, Endurance, and Write Disturb]]\n\nZhou Y et al., IEEE EDL 2024\n\n**Supports**: Advanced writing strategies, error reduction 6×, endurance 100× improvement","x":0,"y":1050,"width":550,"height":200,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"paper5","type":"text","text":"[5] [[Attention-in-Memory for Few-Shot Learning with Configurable Ferroelectric FET Arrays]]\n\nReis D et al., ASPDAC 2021\n\n**Supports**: Hardware adaptive learning, 95.14% accuracy, configurable complexity","x":600,"y":1050,"width":550,"height":200,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"paper6","type":"text","text":"[6] [[Ferroelectric FET Based In-Memory Computing for Few-Shot Learning]]\n\nLaguna AF et al., GLSVLSI 2019\n\n**Supports**: FeFET few-shot learning, prototypical networks, edge efficiency","x":1200,"y":1050,"width":550,"height":200,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"paper7","type":"text","text":"[7] [[Continual Learning With Neuromorphic Computing: Foundations, Methods, and Emerging Applications]]\n\nPutra MFMRVW et al., IEEE Access 2025\n\n**Supports**: Software CL methods, regularization/replay/architecture approaches, computational costs","x":0,"y":1300,"width":550,"height":200,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"paper8","type":"text","text":"[8] [[An in-memory computing architecture based on a duplex two-dimensional material structure for in situ learning]]\n\nNing H et al., Nature 2024\n\n**Supports**: In-situ TIIO architecture, 99.86% accuracy, hardware overhead demonstration","x":600,"y":1300,"width":550,"height":200,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"adaptability-demand","type":"text","text":"**Challenge: Algorithm Adaptability**\n\nEdge devices must operate independently in complex and variable environments, detached from the management and support of central servers, which imposes higher demands on the adaptability and learning capabilities of their algorithms.","x":700,"y":400,"width":600,"height":300,"color":"4"}
	],
